#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7542c69-109b-5a14-9394-be91f8d9c21a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455461 #edb/ref 1071 "Sandra is reading a book. She can read $\\frac{3}{4}$ of a page in one minute. Compute how many pages she can read in $10$ minutes. Between what two whole numbers does your answer lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "611c1b55-08a1-5be9-bcc2-c81ee9ccd015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455461 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out how many pages she can read in $10$ minutes, we need to multiply $\\frac{3}{4}$ by $10.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n10\\times \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{10\\times 3}{4}=\\frac{30}{4}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{4}=\\frac{30÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{15}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{15}{2}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15÷2=7\\,\\text{R}\\,1=7\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in $10$ minutes, Sandra can read a total of $7\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ pages of the book, which lies between $7$ and $8$ pages." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455466 #edb/ref 1071 "An electrician takes a $4$-meter-long electrical cable from which he has to cut a piece that is $\\frac{5}{6}$ of its length. How long will that piece be?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69f7ec22-029d-5e0b-8220-6e82cfcbf15b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455466 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out the length of the piece to be cut, we need to multiply $4$ by $\\frac{5}{6}.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}\\times 4=\\frac{5\\times 4}{6}=\\frac{20}{6}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{6}=\\frac{20÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{10}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{10}{3}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n10÷3=3\\,\\text{R}\\,1=3\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the piece will be $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ long." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455479 #edb/ref 1071 "George rides his bike $\\frac{9}{10}$ of a mile each day, going from home to school and back. Compute how many miles he rides in $5$ days just by going to school and back. Between what two whole numbers does your answer lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "963a7b5f-c364-5634-90c3-78590b2f0ada" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455479 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out how many kilometers George travels in $5$ days just by going to school and back, we have to multiply $\\frac{9}{10}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{5\\times 9}{10}=\\frac{45}{10}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{10}=\\frac{45÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{9}{2}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9÷2=4\\,\\text{R}\\,1=4\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in $5$ days, George rides a total of $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi},$ which lies between $4$ and $5$ miles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455488 #edb/ref 1071 "Theresa pours out $\\frac{7}{8}$ of the contents of an $8$-cup jug of orange juice for her family's breakfast. How many cups of orange juice did Theresa pour from the jug?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbc6d383-8d08-5a4a-80f4-71f8615d00ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455488 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out how many cups of orange juice Theresa poured from the jug, we need to multiply $8$ by $\\frac{7}{8}.$ \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{8}\\times 8=\\frac{7\\times 8}{8}=\\frac{56}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{56}{8}=56÷8=7\n$$\n\nSo, Theresa poured $7$ cups of orange juice from the jug." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
